Explain the context and significance of the Rostker v. Goldberg case in relation to the US armed forces' personnel selection process.
Based on the provided context, I will use my internal knowledge to answer the sub-question.

The Rostker v. Goldberg case (1971) was a landmark Supreme Court decision that challenged the male-only draft in the United States. The case involved a challenge to the Military Selective Service Act, which required all male U.S. citizens between the ages of 18 and 26 to register for the draft, while exempting women from the draft.

The significance of the Rostker v. Goldberg case lies in its impact on the US armed forces' personnel selection process. The court ruled that the male-only draft was unconstitutional, as it discriminated against women. The decision effectively ended the male-only draft and paved the way for the eventual inclusion of women in the military.

In 2015, the Selective Service System was amended to allow women to register for the draft, and in 2019, the US Department of Defense announced plans to open all combat positions to women. The Rostker v. Goldberg case remains a significant milestone in the history of the US military's personnel selection process, as it marked a major shift towards greater equality and inclusivity in the armed forces.
