What is the historical context of the Seminole people and their association with black Indians?
The Seminole people, particularly in Spanish Florida, allied with free blacks and escaped slaves (called maroons) who had African ancestry. This alliance led to the formation of the Black Seminoles, who were mostly blood descendants of Seminole Indians, free blacks, and escaped slaves. Historically, the Black Seminoles lived in distinct bands near the Native American Seminole groups, with some being held as slaves or freedmen due to the stigma of having dark skin.
