What is the historical context and significance of the name 'Mountbatten-Windsor' in the European royal dynasties?
The name 'Mountbatten-Windsor' has its roots in the British royal family's history of surname usage. Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, was born into the Battenberg family, a morganatic branch of the House of Hesse-Darmstadt. When he married Princess Elizabeth (later Queen Elizabeth II), he renounced his Greek titles and took the surname Mountbatten, which is an anglicized form of Battenberg.

In 1960, a declaration was made in the Privy Council that the name 'Mountbatten-Windsor' would be used by male-line descendants of Queen Elizabeth II and Prince Philip who do not have royal styles and titles. This name combines the surname Mountbatten (from Prince Philip's family) with Windsor (the surname of the British royal family).

The significance of the name 'Mountbatten-Windsor' lies in its connection to the British royal family's history and the blending of different European royal dynasties. It reflects the complex family relationships and surname changes that have occurred within the royal family over the centuries.
